<h2>What veneers can fix</h2>
<ul class="checklist">
  <li>Stained or discolored teeth that don't respond to whitening</li>
  <li>Chipped, worn, or short front teeth</li>
  <li>Small gaps you'd like to close</li>
  <li>Slightly crowded or rotated front teeth (when aligners aren't preferred)</li>
  <li>An overall smile that feels uneven or asymmetric</li>
</ul>

<h2>The veneer process</h2>
<ol class="checklist" style="list-style:decimal;padding-left:28px">
  <li><strong>Design.</strong> We talk through what you want, take photos and impressions, and create a digital or wax mock-up of your future smile.</li>
  <li><strong>Try-in.</strong> You see and approve the shape before any tooth preparation begins.</li>
  <li><strong>Preparation &amp; temporaries.</strong> A very small amount of enamel is shaped. You leave wearing comfortable temporary veneers.</li>
  <li><strong>Final bonding.</strong> A few weeks later, your custom porcelain veneers are bonded into place.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Veneers vs. crowns vs. bonding</h2>
<p>If you're looking at small chips or a single discolored tooth, simple bonding may be the right answer. If a tooth is heavily cracked or already has a large filling, a crown is more appropriate. Veneers sit in the middle: bigger change than bonding, more conservative than a crown. We'll be honest about which option is right for your situation.</p>
